  "worked_solution": "To find the $x$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{5}-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{7} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0+2)^{2}}{5}-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{7} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{7} & =1 \\\\ -\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{7} & =1-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{7} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =-\\frac{7}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot take the square root of a negative number. Therefore, the equation has no real solutions.\nSo, there are no $x$-intercepts.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
